-- CISSP D2 Asset Security expansion, part A of 2 (objectives 2.1-2.3; sibling 2.4-2.6 covered separately). New content: 2.1 classify assets (inventory, valuation, FIPS 199 categorization, high-water mark, marking, declassification review); 2.2 information/asset ownership (owner/custodian/steward distinctions, GDPR controller/processor, accountability non-delegation, DPO); 2.3 protect privacy (minimization, purpose limitation, Privacy by Design, anonymization vs pseudonymization, DPIA). New ids: ci-q-600..636, ci-f-600..629, ci-a-220..229, ci-y-130..135.

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 2.1 classify information and assets
('ci-q-600','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Which statement best explains why a complete and accurate asset inventory is a prerequisite for effective classification?$q$,$q$["It replaces the need for a data owner to assign classification levels","An asset cannot be classified, valued, or protected if the organization does not even know it exists","It is required only for hardware, never for information assets","It is a one-time project that never needs to be revisited"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A classification program can only protect what it knows about. Asset inventory identifies every asset - its location, type, and owner - so a classification level, value, and protection requirement can even be assigned; it doesn't substitute for the owner's classification decision, and inventories must be kept current, not treated as a one-time project.$q$),
('ci-q-601','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following is NOT a legitimate criterion for determining an information asset's classification level?$q$,$q$["The asset's replacement cost or business value","Legal, regulatory, or contractual obligations tied to the asset","The name of the vendor who manufactured the storage hardware","The age and useful remaining life of the information"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Classification criteria focus on the asset's value, sensitivity, criticality, legal/regulatory obligations, and useful life - factors tied to the impact of loss or disclosure. The hardware vendor's name has no bearing on how sensitive the information itself is.$q$),
('ci-q-602','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A security manager assigns a data asset's value using concrete dollar figures, such as replacement cost and projected lost revenue if the asset were compromised. Which asset valuation approach is this?$q$,$q$["Qualitative valuation","Nominal valuation","Quantitative valuation","Depreciation-based valuation"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Quantitative valuation expresses asset value in objective, measurable terms like dollars - replacement cost, lost revenue, or regulatory fines. Qualitative valuation instead uses relative rankings (critical/high/moderate/low) when hard numbers aren't available.$q$),
('ci-q-603','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$When precise dollar figures are unavailable, an organization instead ranks an asset's importance using terms like "critical," "high," "moderate," and "low" based on expert judgment. This is an example of:$q$,$q$["Quantitative valuation","Annualized valuation","Qualitative valuation","Residual valuation"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Qualitative valuation uses subjective, relative rankings rather than objective dollar figures, useful when precise financial impact is hard to calculate. Quantitative valuation is the dollar-based counterpart; "annualized" and "residual" describe risk concepts, not valuation methods.$q$),
('ci-q-604','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Under FIPS 199, a system whose loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability would be expected to have a severe or catastrophic adverse effect on organizational operations is categorized at which impact level for that objective?$q$,$q$["Moderate","Critical","Low","High"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$FIPS 199 defines only three potential-impact levels - Low, Moderate, and High - rated separately for confidentiality, integrity, and availability. "Severe or catastrophic adverse effect" is the defined threshold for High; "Critical" is not one of the three official FIPS 199 levels.$q$),
('ci-q-605','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A file server stores a mix of Public, Internal, and Confidential documents in shared folders with no technical separation by sensitivity. Applying the classification high-water-mark principle, at what level must the server itself be protected?$q$,$q$["Internal, the average level of the data it stores","Public, the lowest level of any data it stores","No classification is needed until the data is separated","Confidential, the highest level of any data it stores"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$The high-water-mark principle holds that when data of different sensitivities is commingled without reliable technical separation, the entire system or container must be protected at the highest classification level present - averaging or defaulting to the lowest level would under-protect the most sensitive data it holds.$q$),
('ci-q-606','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$What is the primary purpose of classification marking, such as banner headers, footers, or cover sheets, on documents and media?$q$,$q$["To automatically encrypt the document","To satisfy an aesthetic branding requirement","To visibly communicate the required handling and protection level to anyone who accesses it","To replace the need for access controls"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Marking exists to make classification visible and actionable to anyone who handles the asset, so they immediately know what handling and protection rules apply. It is not a technical control itself (it doesn't encrypt anything or replace access controls) - it's a communication mechanism that supports the controls people and systems must still apply.$q$),
('ci-q-607','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$Why do mature classification programs require periodic review of an asset's assigned classification level rather than treating it as permanent?$q$,$q$["Only hardware assets require reclassification, not information","Regulations require reclassification at exactly the same fixed hour every day","Classification levels never actually need to change once set","Sensitivity and value can change over time, so the level may need to be upgraded or downgraded"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$An asset's sensitivity is not static: information can age out of relevance, a product can ship and stop being a trade secret, or a new regulation can newly cover data that wasn't previously regulated. Periodic or trigger-based review lets an organization declassify (downgrade) or reclassify (upgrade) an asset so its protection level continues to match its actual risk.$q$),
('ci-q-608','cissp',2,'2.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO items that are legitimate inputs into determining an information asset's classification level. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["The potential impact of unauthorized disclosure on the organization","The physical color of the folder the data happens to be stored in","Legal or regulatory obligations that apply to the data","How many years ago the storage cabinet was purchased"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Classification level should be driven by factors tied to actual risk and obligation: the impact of unauthorized disclosure and any legal/regulatory/contractual duties attached to the data. Physical, cosmetic, or unrelated procurement details have no bearing on an asset's sensitivity.$q$),
('ci-q-609','cissp',2,'2.1','multi',3,$q$An organization's asset inventory should capture which TWO of the following for each classified information asset? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["The personal opinion of every employee who has ever accessed it","The designated owner accountable for the asset","The exact retail price of the nearest office chair","The assigned classification level and applicable handling requirements"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A useful asset inventory record ties each asset to an accountable owner and documents its classification level and the handling requirements that follow from it - the information needed to protect and govern the asset. Unrelated or subjective details add no protective value.$q$),
('ci-q-610','cissp',2,'2.1','ordering',1,$q$Order the steps of a typical asset classification process from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Identify and inventory the asset","Determine the asset's value and potential impact if compromised","Assign a classification level based on defined criteria","Apply classification labels and handling requirements","Periodically review and update the classification"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Classification begins with knowing the asset exists (inventory), then assessing its value/impact, then assigning a level against defined criteria, then marking it and applying the handling controls that level requires, and finally revisiting that assignment periodically as circumstances change.$q$),
('ci-q-611','cissp',2,'2.1','matching',2,$q$Match each classification-related term to its best description.$q$,$q${"left":["Asset inventory","Classification criteria","High-water mark","Declassification review"],"right":["A comprehensive record of the assets an organization owns and must protect","The factors, such as value, sensitivity, and legal duty, used to decide an asset's protection level","The rule that a system holding mixed-sensitivity data is protected at its most sensitive level","A periodic reassessment that may downgrade or upgrade an asset's assigned classification"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four concepts work together across the classification lifecycle: inventory identifies what must be classified, criteria determine the level, the high-water mark governs mixed-sensitivity systems, and declassification review keeps the assignment current over time.$q$),
-- 2.2 information and asset ownership
('ci-q-612','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Which role is responsible for the day-to-day technical implementation of protection controls, such as performing backups, applying encryption, and provisioning access, as directed by the data owner?$q$,$q$["Data owner","Data subject","Data custodian","Data controller"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$The data custodian carries out the hands-on technical safeguards the owner directs - backups, encryption, patching, access provisioning - without setting classification or access policy, which remains the owner's decision.$q$),
('ci-q-613','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Which role is primarily responsible for the quality, accuracy, and correct business context or metadata of a data asset, rather than its technical protection?$q$,$q$["Data custodian","Data steward","System administrator","Data processor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The data steward focuses on whether the data is accurate, consistent, and correctly understood in its business context (definitions, metadata, proper use) - a distinct concern from the custodian's job of technically safeguarding it.$q$),
('ci-q-614','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A marketing analytics vendor processes a company's customer data strictly according to the company's written instructions and has no independent authority over why or how the data is used. Which GDPR role does the vendor occupy?$q$,$q$["Data subject","Data controller","Data protection officer","Data processor"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$A party that processes personal data only on another party's documented instructions, without independently deciding the purpose or means of processing, is acting as a data processor under GDPR - a distinct role from the controller, who retains that decision-making authority.$q$),
('ci-q-615','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$What distinguishes a "system owner" from a "data owner" in an asset ownership program?$q$,$q$["They are two names for exactly the same role with no real distinction","The system owner is accountable for the platform or infrastructure hosting the data, while the data owner is accountable for the information's classification and use","The system owner has no security responsibilities at all","The data owner is always a member of the IT department"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The system owner is accountable for the platform or infrastructure itself (its patching, configuration, uptime), while the data owner is accountable for the information hosted on that platform (its classification, access policy, acceptable use). A single system may host data belonging to several different data owners.$q$),
('ci-q-616','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$An organization assigns a business executive as the accountable owner of a customer database, while a database administration team performs the actual backups, patching, and access provisioning. If a breach occurs because of a missed patch, who remains ultimately accountable?$q$,$q$["No one, because the task was delegated","The end user, because they accessed the data","The database administration team, because they performed the technical task","The data owner, because accountability cannot be delegated, only the underlying task can"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Ownership models distinguish delegating a task from delegating accountability: the owner can hand the technical work to a custodian, but remains the accountable party for the outcome. The custodian's failure to patch does not shift accountability away from the owner who directed and is answerable for the asset.$q$),
('ci-q-617','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$A cloud analytics vendor under contract with a company discovers it has begun using the company's customer data for its own additional purposes, beyond what its contract instructs. What is the vendor's status for that additional processing?$q$,$q$["It becomes the data subject for that processing","It remains a data processor with no additional obligations","Nothing changes, because vendors can never become controllers","It effectively becomes a data controller for that processing and takes on controller-level accountability for it"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$A processor's protected status depends on acting only within the controller's instructions. The moment it independently decides to use the data for its own purposes, it is determining the purpose and means of that processing - which is the legal definition of a controller - and it takes on controller-level accountability for that portion of the processing.$q$),
('ci-q-618','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Which statement correctly distinguishes a data owner's responsibility from a data custodian's responsibility?$q$,$q$["Both roles are identical and interchangeable in every organization","Only the custodian can ever be held accountable for a breach","The custodian decides classification while the owner performs backups","The owner decides classification, access policy, and acceptable use; the custodian carries out the technical safeguards the owner directs"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$The owner sets policy-level decisions - classification, who may access the asset, and how it may be used - while the custodian executes the technical protections (backups, encryption, access lists) that implement those decisions day to day.$q$),
('ci-q-619','cissp',2,'2.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO responsibilities that typically belong to a data custodian rather than a data owner. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Deciding the data's classification level","Performing routine backups of the data","Applying encryption and access controls as directed","Approving who is authorized to access the data at a policy level"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$The custodian performs the technical safeguards the owner directs - backups and applying encryption/access controls - while decisions about classification level and policy-level access approval remain with the owner, who is accountable for the asset.$q$),
('ci-q-620','cissp',2,'2.2','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe the GDPR data controller versus data processor distinction. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Any entity that merely touches personal data automatically becomes the controller","The controller determines the purposes and means of processing","The processor bears sole accountability while the controller bears none","The processor acts only on the documented instructions of the controller"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The controller is the party that decides why and how personal data is processed, while the processor is bound to act strictly on the controller's documented instructions. Merely handling data doesn't automatically make an entity a controller, and accountability is not the processor's alone - the controller bears primary accountability, though processors carry some direct statutory obligations too.$q$),
('ci-q-621','cissp',2,'2.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical steps an organization follows when establishing ownership and custodial responsibility for a new data asset, from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Identify and formally designate a business owner accountable for the asset","The owner defines the asset's classification, access policy, and handling requirements","The owner delegates day-to-day technical protection tasks to a custodian","The custodian implements the directed technical controls, such as backups, encryption, and access provisioning","The owner periodically reviews that the custodial controls still meet policy"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Ownership is established before controls are assigned: a business owner is designated and made accountable, that owner then defines the policy the asset must follow, delegates the hands-on work to a custodian, the custodian carries out the directed controls, and the owner closes the loop with ongoing review that those controls still satisfy policy.$q$),
('ci-q-622','cissp',2,'2.2','matching',2,$q$Match each role to the responsibility that best defines it.$q$,$q${"left":["Data owner","Data custodian","Data steward","Data controller (GDPR)"],"right":["Accountable executive who sets classification and access policy for a data asset","Implements the technical safeguards the owner directs, such as backups, encryption, and provisioning","Ensures the data's quality, accuracy, and correct business context or metadata","Determines the purposes and means of processing personal data and bears primary GDPR accountability"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four roles divide accountability, execution, quality, and legal purpose-setting into distinct responsibilities so that classification, protection, usability, and privacy compliance are each clearly owned rather than falling through the cracks.$q$),
('ci-q-623','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$An organization has both a designated data owner for its customer database and a Data Protection Officer (DPO) overseeing GDPR compliance. What best describes the DPO's role relative to that data owner?$q$,$q$["The DPO replaces the data owner as the accountable party for that specific database","The DPO is an independent oversight and advisory role monitoring privacy compliance across the organization, distinct from being the accountable business owner of any single data asset","The DPO and data owner are the same role under two different titles","The DPO's only job is to perform the technical backups the data owner directs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The DPO is a governance and advisory function required under GDPR for certain organizations - monitoring compliance, advising on data protection obligations and impact assessments, and liaising with regulators - rather than being the accountable owner of any individual data asset, a role that stays with the designated business owner.$q$),
-- 2.3 protect privacy
('ci-q-624','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$What is "data minimization" as a privacy principle?$q$,$q$["Deleting all data immediately after collection regardless of purpose","Storing data in the smallest possible file format","Collecting and retaining only the personal data actually necessary for a specified purpose","Encrypting all personal data at rest"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Data minimization means collecting, processing, and retaining only the personal data genuinely necessary to accomplish a stated purpose - reducing exposure and breach impact by never holding data "just in case" it might be useful later.$q$),
('ci-q-625','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$What is "purpose limitation" as a privacy principle?$q$,$q$["Data must be limited to a maximum file size","Only privileged users may ever view personal data","Personal data can only be accessed a limited number of times before deletion","Personal data collected for one stated purpose should not be reused for a materially different, incompatible purpose without new justification or consent"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Purpose limitation constrains how already-collected data may be reused: data gathered for a specific stated purpose should not later be repurposed for a materially different use unless there is a fresh legal basis or the individual consents again.$q$),
('ci-q-626','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$Which Privacy by Design principle states that privacy protections should be the automatic, out-of-the-box setting rather than something a user must opt into?$q$,$q$["Full functionality (positive-sum)","Privacy as the default setting","End-to-end security","Visibility and transparency"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$"Privacy as the default setting" means the most privacy-protective configuration is already active when a system ships - individuals shouldn't have to locate a settings menu and manually opt in to protect their own data.$q$),
('ci-q-627','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Which Privacy by Design principle holds that privacy should be built into the architecture of systems and business practices from the start, rather than added afterward?$q$,$q$["Respect for user privacy","Proactive not reactive","Privacy embedded into design","Full functionality"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$"Privacy embedded into design" calls for privacy to be a core architectural component from the outset - part of the system's design and business processes - rather than a compliance patch bolted on after the system already exists.$q$),
('ci-q-628','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization strips all identifiers from a dataset using a transformation that is technically irreversible - it is impossible, even for the organization itself, to re-link any record back to a specific individual. What has been achieved?$q$,$q$["Pseudonymization","Tokenization for reversible masking","Encryption at rest","Anonymization"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Anonymization irreversibly removes or transforms identifying elements so that no one, including the data holder, can re-link the data to an individual. This is distinct from pseudonymization or reversible tokenization, where a key exists somewhere that could restore identity.$q$),
('ci-q-629','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A researcher replaces direct identifiers, like name and government ID number, with a reversible code, while keeping a separately secured mapping table that would let the original identity be restored if legally necessary. What technique is this?$q$,$q$["Purpose limitation","Data destruction","Anonymization","Pseudonymization"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with a token or code while preserving a securely held key or mapping table that allows authorized re-identification later - unlike anonymization, which is deliberately irreversible.$q$),
('ci-q-630','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$Under GDPR, which statement correctly describes the treatment of pseudonymized versus truly anonymized data?$q$,$q$["Anonymized data still requires the same consent obligations as raw personal data","Pseudonymization and anonymization are legally identical and interchangeable under GDPR","Both pseudonymized and anonymized data are always fully exempt from GDPR","Pseudonymized data is still considered personal data because re-identification remains possible; truly (irreversibly) anonymized data falls outside GDPR's scope"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Because pseudonymized data can still be re-linked to an individual using the retained key, GDPR continues to treat it as personal data subject to its protections. Truly anonymized data - where re-identification is no longer possible by any means reasonably likely to be used - is no longer considered personal data and falls outside the regulation's scope.$q$),
('ci-q-631','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary purpose of conducting a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) before launching a new high-risk personal-data processing activity?$q$,$q$["To calculate the annualized loss expectancy of a potential data breach","To satisfy a one-time marketing requirement","To systematically identify and mitigate privacy risks to individuals before processing begins","To replace the need for a data classification policy"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A DPIA is a structured risk-assessment process specifically aimed at identifying and mitigating privacy risks to individuals before a new, likely-high-risk processing activity begins - distinct from financial risk calculations like ALE or the separate discipline of data classification.$q$),
('ci-q-632','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following is an example of "special category" (sensitive) personal data that typically requires heightened privacy protection?$q$,$q$["A company's public press release","Health and medical information","A publicly listed corporate phone number","A product's public marketing brochure"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Special/sensitive categories of personal data - such as health status, racial or ethnic origin, religious belief, sexual orientation, and biometric data - carry a materially higher risk of harm or discrimination if exposed, so privacy frameworks impose stricter processing conditions on them than on ordinary public-facing business information.$q$),
('ci-q-633','cissp',2,'2.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO practices that reflect the Privacy by Design philosophy. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Collecting the maximum amount of data upfront in case it becomes useful later","Building privacy safeguards into a system's architecture from the initial design phase","Setting the most privacy-protective option as the default configuration","Adding privacy controls only after a regulator issues a fine"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Privacy by Design is proactive: it embeds privacy safeguards into the architecture from the outset and defaults to the most privacy-protective configuration, rather than reacting only after harm (like a fine) has already occurred or maximizing data collection just in case.$q$),
('ci-q-634','cissp',2,'2.3','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO correct distinctions between anonymization and pseudonymization. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Pseudonymized data is exempt from all privacy regulation","Anonymization is irreversible; no key exists that could re-identify the individual","Anonymized data is always still considered personal data under GDPR","Pseudonymization is reversible via a securely stored mapping or key"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Anonymization is deliberately irreversible - no key exists to undo it - which is exactly why truly anonymized data falls outside GDPR's definition of personal data. Pseudonymization, by contrast, is reversible through a securely held mapping or key, which is why pseudonymized data remains regulated personal data.$q$),
('ci-q-635','cissp',2,'2.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical steps of conducting a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Describe the proposed processing activity and its purpose","Assess the necessity and proportionality of the processing against that purpose","Identify and evaluate risks to individuals' rights and freedoms","Identify measures to mitigate the identified risks","Document the outcome and consult the DPO or regulator if high risk remains"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$A DPIA starts by describing what the processing is and why, checks whether it's actually necessary and proportionate to that purpose, then identifies and evaluates the resulting risks to individuals, proposes mitigations for those risks, and finishes by documenting the outcome and escalating to the DPO or regulator if significant risk remains despite mitigation.$q$),
('ci-q-636','cissp',2,'2.3','matching',1,$q$Match each privacy concept to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["Data minimization","Purpose limitation","Anonymization","Pseudonymization"],"right":["Collecting and retaining only the personal data necessary for a stated purpose","Not reusing already-collected data for a new, incompatible purpose without justification","Irreversibly stripping data of identifiers so re-identification is not possible","Replacing identifiers with a reversible token protected by a separately held key"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four privacy-engineering concepts work together: minimization and purpose limitation govern how much data is collected and how it may be used, while anonymization and pseudonymization are the two technical de-identification techniques - one irreversible, one reversible - used to reduce privacy risk in the data itself.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 2.1 classify information and assets (core)
('ci-f-600','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$Why is an accurate asset inventory foundational to information classification?$$,$$A classification program can only protect what it knows exists. Asset inventory identifies each asset's location, type, and owner, providing the prerequisite for assigning a value, classification level, and protection requirements - it doesn't substitute for the owner's classification decision itself.$$),
('ci-f-601','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What criteria are commonly used to determine an asset's classification level?$$,$$Its value or replacement cost, sensitivity and criticality to operations, age and remaining useful life, and any legal, regulatory, or contractual obligations attached to it. These factors combine to indicate the impact of unauthorized disclosure, modification, or loss.$$),
('ci-f-602','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between quantitative and qualitative asset valuation?$$,$$Quantitative valuation expresses an asset's worth in objective, measurable terms such as replacement cost or lost revenue. Qualitative valuation instead uses relative, judgment-based rankings like critical/high/moderate/low when precise figures aren't available.$$),
('ci-f-603','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What does FIPS 199 impact-level categorization measure, and what are its three levels?$$,$$It measures the potential adverse impact on an organization if a system's confidentiality, integrity, or availability were compromised, rated separately for each objective as Low, Moderate, or High. The system's overall categorization for each objective takes the highest applicable rating.$$),
('ci-f-604','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the classification "high-water mark" principle?$$,$$When a system, document, or repository holds data of multiple classification levels without reliable technical separation, the entire container must be protected at the highest classification level present - because it cannot be guaranteed that the more sensitive data is adequately isolated from lower-level access.$$),
('ci-f-605','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the purpose of classification marking or labeling?$$,$$To visibly (via banners, headers, footers, or cover sheets) or programmatically (via metadata tags) communicate an asset's classification level and required handling to anyone who accesses it - it's a communication mechanism, not a substitute for the technical controls that level requires.$$),
('ci-f-606','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$Why must classification levels be periodically reviewed rather than assigned once and forgotten?$$,$$Sensitivity and value change over time - information can age out of relevance, a product can ship and lose its trade-secret status, or a new regulation can newly cover data it didn't before. Periodic or trigger-based review lets an organization downgrade or upgrade the classification so protection stays proportionate to actual risk.$$),
('ci-f-607','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$Besides information, what other categories of assets does a classification program need to inventory and protect?$$,$$Hardware and physical devices, software and licenses, and intangible assets such as intellectual property, trade secrets, and brand reputation - classification and asset-protection efforts extend beyond data itself.$$),
('ci-f-608','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between classifying information and categorizing an information system, as NIST distinguishes the terms?$$,$$Classification assigns a sensitivity level to the information itself, such as Confidential. Categorization (for example, under FIPS 199) rates an information system's potential impact if its confidentiality, integrity, or availability were compromised, which then drives which security control baseline the system requires.$$),
('ci-f-609','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What should a formal data/asset classification policy define?$$,$$The classification criteria used to assign a level, the defined levels and their meaning, required labeling and marking, handling and access requirements per level, the roles responsible for assigning and reviewing classification, and the schedule for periodic review or reclassification.$$),
-- 2.1 acronyms
('ci-a-220','cissp','acronym',2,'2.1',$$FIPS$$,$$Federal Information Processing Standards - U.S. government standards, such as FIPS 199, used to categorize information systems by potential impact.$$),
('ci-a-221','cissp','acronym',2,'2.1',$$CMDB$$,$$Configuration Management Database - a repository of asset records, including hardware, software, and their relationships, that underpins accurate inventory and classification tracking.$$),
('ci-a-222','cissp','acronym',2,'2.1',$$ITAM$$,$$IT Asset Management - the discipline of tracking and managing the lifecycle, value, and classification of an organization's technology assets.$$),
-- 2.1 feynman
('ci-y-130','cissp','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain why a system holding a mix of Public and Confidential data must be protected as if everything on it were Confidential.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a shared drive where most files are harmless public marketing material, but a handful of Confidential contracts also live there with no separate access controls. If I protect the drive only at the "average" or "lowest" level, anyone who can read the public files can also read the Confidential ones - the weakest link determines what's actually exposed. Since I usually can't cheaply guarantee perfect technical separation between files of different sensitivity on the same system, the safest and most honest approach is to treat the whole system as being at its highest contained sensitivity - the high-water mark. It's more conservative and sometimes more expensive, but it avoids silently under-protecting the most sensitive thing on the system.$$),
('ci-y-131','cissp','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain how FIPS 199 impact categorization differs from labeling a single document Confidential.$$,$$Model answer: Labeling a document Confidential is a judgment about that one piece of information's sensitivity - if disclosed, how much harm would it cause? FIPS 199 categorization works one level up: it rates an entire information system by the worst-case impact if its confidentiality, integrity, or availability were compromised, scored separately as Low, Moderate, or High for each of the three. A system can end up categorized High even if no single document on it is labeled with the highest classification, because the categorization considers the system's overall mission impact, not just one file's label. That system-level categorization is what later determines which security control baseline the system must implement - the classification of individual data feeds into it but isn't the same measurement.$$),
-- 2.2 information and asset ownership (core)
('ci-f-610','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$Name concrete tasks that fall under a data custodian's technical responsibilities.$$,$$Performing routine backups, applying encryption, patching systems, and provisioning or de-provisioning access - all carried out at the data owner's direction. The custodian executes technical safeguards; it does not decide classification or access policy.$$),
('ci-f-611','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What is a data steward accountable for that neither the owner nor the custodian focuses on?$$,$$The data's quality, accuracy, consistency, and correct business context or metadata - making sure the data means what people think it means and remains fit for its intended business use. This is distinct from protecting it (the custodian's job) or setting its policy (the owner's job).$$),
('ci-f-612','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What specific obligations does GDPR place directly on a data processor, distinct from the controller's obligations?$$,$$A processor must act only on the controller's documented instructions, implement appropriate technical and organizational security measures, assist the controller with breach notification and data-subject requests, and not engage a sub-processor without the controller's authorization. The controller separately bears primary accountability for the lawfulness of the processing itself.$$),
('ci-f-613','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$Can accountability for a data asset be delegated the way tasks can?$$,$$No. An owner can delegate day-to-day tasks, such as backups or patching, to a custodian, but remains accountable for the outcome. Accountability stays with the designated owner even when someone else performs the underlying work.$$),
('ci-f-614','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the difference between a system owner and a data owner?$$,$$The system owner is accountable for the platform or infrastructure that hosts and processes data - its patching, configuration, and uptime. The data owner is accountable for the information itself - its classification, access policy, and acceptable use. A single system can host data belonging to several different data owners.$$),
('ci-f-615','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What happens under GDPR if a data processor starts using personal data for its own purposes beyond the controller's instructions?$$,$$For that additional processing, the vendor stops acting merely as a processor and takes on controller-level accountability, because it is now independently determining the purpose of that processing rather than simply following instructions.$$),
('ci-f-616','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$Why does an asset ownership program typically assign one accountable owner per data asset rather than a group?$$,$$Shared or group accountability tends to dilute responsibility - "everyone's job" can become no one's job. A single named, accountable owner ensures there is clear decision-making authority over classification and access, even though many people may perform delegated tasks.$$),
('ci-f-617','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What does a RACI-style responsibility matrix add to an ownership program?$$,$$It explicitly documents who is Responsible (does the work), Accountable (owns the outcome - exactly one party), Consulted (provides input), and Informed (kept updated) for each data-related task, clarifying overlapping owner, custodian, and steward duties and closing gaps between them.$$),
('ci-f-618','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What role does a Data Protection Officer play relative to individual data owners under GDPR?$$,$$The DPO is an independent oversight and advisory role that monitors privacy compliance organization-wide, advises on data protection obligations and impact assessments, and serves as a contact point for regulators and data subjects. It is a governance function, not the accountable business owner of any single data asset.$$),
('ci-f-619','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$Why must ownership be assigned even for data an organization doesn't store itself, such as data held by a third-party processor?$$,$$Data disclosed to a processor or held in a SaaS platform still needs an accountable owner to set its classification, approve who may access it, and verify the processor is meeting its contractual and regulatory obligations - ownership doesn't end just because storage or processing is outsourced.$$),
-- 2.2 acronyms
('ci-a-223','cissp','acronym',2,'2.2',$$DPO$$,$$Data Protection Officer - an independent, GDPR-mandated role overseeing an organization's data-protection compliance and serving as a contact point for regulators and data subjects.$$),
('ci-a-224','cissp','acronym',2,'2.2',$$CPO$$,$$Chief Privacy Officer - a senior executive who owns an organization's overall privacy strategy and program, distinct from the statutory DPO role.$$),
('ci-a-225','cissp','acronym',2,'2.2',$$RACI$$,$$Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed - a matrix clarifying who performs the work versus who is ultimately accountable for a data asset or task.$$),
-- 2.2 feynman
('ci-y-132','cissp','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain why "accountability can't be delegated, only tasks can" matters for data ownership.$$,$$Model answer: Say I'm the designated owner of a customer database, and I hand the backups and patching to an IT team. If a breach happens because a patch was missed, it would be easy to think the IT team is now "responsible" and I'm off the hook - but that's not how ownership works. I delegated the doing, not the owning. I chose to hand that task to that team, I'm supposed to be checking that they're doing it, and if it goes wrong, the organization still looks to me as the accountable party, because I was the one with the authority to set requirements and verify compliance. If accountability could be quietly handed off along with the task, no one with real decision-making power would ever be on the hook for anything - which is exactly the gap ownership models are designed to close.$$),
('ci-y-133','cissp','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain the difference between a data controller and a data processor using a concrete SaaS vendor example.$$,$$Model answer: Suppose my company hires a SaaS email-marketing vendor to send campaigns to our customer list. We decide who gets emailed, what the campaigns say, and how long we keep the list - that decision-making role makes us the controller. The vendor only sends what we tell it to send, using our list, under our instructions, and can't decide to reuse that list for its own separate marketing - that makes it the processor. If the vendor ever started using our customer list for its own unrelated purposes without asking us, it would step outside the processor role for that use and start acting like a controller for it, taking on that accountability itself. The test isn't who physically touches the data; it's who decides why and how it's used.$$),
-- 2.3 protect privacy (core)
('ci-f-620','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is data minimization?$$,$$The principle of collecting, processing, and retaining only the personal data actually necessary to accomplish a specified, legitimate purpose - reducing exposure, storage cost, and breach impact by never holding data "just in case" it might become useful.$$),
('ci-f-621','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is purpose limitation?$$,$$Personal data collected for a specific, stated purpose should not be repurposed for a new, materially different use without a fresh legal basis or the individual's consent. It constrains how already-collected data may later be reused, distinct from the collection limit set by data minimization.$$),
('ci-f-622','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What are the seven foundational principles of Privacy by Design?$$,$$Proactive not reactive; privacy as the default setting; privacy embedded into design; full functionality (positive-sum, not zero-sum); end-to-end security across the full data lifecycle; visibility and transparency; and respect for user privacy (keep it user-centric).$$),
('ci-f-623','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is the difference between anonymization and pseudonymization?$$,$$Anonymization irreversibly removes or transforms identifiers so no one, even the data holder, can re-link the data to an individual. Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with a token or code while keeping a securely stored key, allowing authorized re-identification later.$$),
('ci-f-624','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$Why does GDPR still treat pseudonymized data as personal data, while anonymized data falls outside its scope?$$,$$Pseudonymized data can still be re-linked to an individual using the retained key or mapping table, so the privacy risk remains. Truly (irreversibly) anonymized data can no longer identify anyone by any reasonably likely means, so it no longer meets the definition of personal data under the regulation.$$),
('ci-f-625','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is a Data Protection Impact Assessment, and when is one required?$$,$$A structured process to identify and mitigate privacy risks to individuals before a new processing activity begins. It's generally required when processing is likely to result in high risk to individuals' rights - for example, large-scale profiling or processing of special-category data.$$),
('ci-f-626','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is "special category" (sensitive) personal data, and why does it get extra protection?$$,$$Data revealing things like health status, racial or ethnic origin, religious belief, sexual orientation, or biometric identifiers. Its exposure carries a materially higher risk of harm or discrimination to the individual, so privacy frameworks impose stricter processing conditions and consent requirements on it than on ordinary personal data.$$),
('ci-f-627','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What does "privacy as the default setting" mean in practice?$$,$$Systems should ship with the most privacy-protective configuration already active - individuals shouldn't have to find a settings menu and manually opt in to protect their own data. Privacy is the automatic starting point, not an optional extra step the user must take.$$),
('ci-f-628','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$How does data minimization reduce breach impact even before any technical control fails?$$,$$If data was never collected, or was deleted once no longer needed, it simply isn't present to be stolen when a breach occurs. Minimization shrinks the potential blast radius of an incident regardless of how strong the surrounding technical controls turn out to be.$$),
('ci-f-629','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is the difference between consent and purpose limitation as privacy safeguards?$$,$$Consent is the legal basis obtained from an individual to process their data for a stated purpose in the first place. Purpose limitation is the ongoing constraint that data, once collected, keeps being used only for that same or a compatible purpose rather than being repurposed later without fresh justification.$$),
-- 2.3 acronyms
('ci-a-226','cissp','acronym',2,'2.3',$$PII$$,$$Personally Identifiable Information - any data that can identify a specific individual, alone or combined with other data; the core subject of privacy-protection obligations.$$),
('ci-a-227','cissp','acronym',2,'2.3',$$PHI$$,$$Protected Health Information - individually identifiable health data protected under HIPAA and similar health-privacy regulations.$$),
('ci-a-228','cissp','acronym',2,'2.3',$$DPIA$$,$$Data Protection Impact Assessment - a structured risk assessment performed before high-risk personal-data processing begins.$$),
('ci-a-229','cissp','acronym',2,'2.3',$$PbD$$,$$Privacy by Design - the philosophy of embedding privacy protections into systems and processes from the outset, rather than adding them after the fact.$$),
-- 2.3 feynman
('ci-y-134','cissp','feynman',2,'2.3',$$Explain the difference between anonymization and pseudonymization using a hospital research example.$$,$$Model answer: Say a hospital wants to share patient records with outside researchers studying a disease. If it anonymizes the data - stripping names, exact birthdates, and any other identifiers and making sure no key exists anywhere to reverse it - the researchers get useful clinical data but can never trace a record back to a real patient, even if they wanted to; the hospital itself couldn't either. If instead the hospital pseudonymizes the data, it replaces the patient's name with a code like "Patient-4471" and keeps a separately secured lookup table linking codes back to real identities. That's useful if the hospital later needs to contact a patient about a significant finding, but it also means the data is still "re-identifiable" in principle, so it remains regulated personal/health data requiring the same protections as the original records - unlike truly anonymized data, which escapes that regulatory burden precisely because it can't be undone.$$),
('ci-y-135','cissp','feynman',2,'2.3',$$Explain why "privacy by design" asks you to build in privacy from day one instead of adding it after a system already meets its functional requirements.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine building an app first, focused purely on features, and only afterward asking "how do we make this privacy-compliant?" By then the data model, the APIs, the third-party integrations, and the UI flows are already locked in - protecting privacy becomes an expensive retrofit, and some things (like data you already over-collected and shared with partners) may not even be fixable after the fact. Privacy by Design flips the order: privacy requirements - minimizing what you collect, defaulting to protective settings, designing consent and deletion flows - are treated as part of the functional requirements from day one, alongside performance and usability. It's the same logic as "shift left" in secure coding: fixing a design flaw before anything is built is far cheaper and more effective than bolting on a fix after the system, and its bad habits, already exist.$$);
